{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>In the evening, and you shall know, etc.</b> The explanation is: since Israel spoke in a manner of conclusively stating their motive — that they would find meat in the wilderness — when they said ״מִי יִתֵּן מוּתֵנוּ״ and so forth, and they had not asked for this thing beforehand — he would declare to them that the thing was impossible for them. Therefore he said to them ״עֶרֶב וִידַעְתֶּם כִּי ה׳״ and so forth: for until now you do not know the One who brought you out — how awesome are His deeds, and you did not take knowledge from the great trials that He is omnipotent; now, anew, you will recognize Him. And the intent of his words is a moral rebuke, for טַח מֵרְאוֹת עֵינֵיהֶם וְגוֹ׳ (Isaiah 44:18). And the reason he did not attach the knowledge except to the event of the evening and not to the event of the morning, is because that is what stood out to them in their motive. And he said <b>and you shall know</b> with the addition of the vav, to say: not only will the fear of death by famine be removed, but it will be in a wondrous manner like the awesome deed of HaShem who brought you out of the land of Egypt."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:28:40.221979884Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}